  "prompt_text": "This schematic links gut bacteria, bile acids, and liver cancer, and shows how antibiotics may interrupt that pathway. The liver, stomach, duodenum, portal vein, and common bile duct are drawn together to emphasize circulation between the intestine and liver. Primary bile acids are shown moving from the liver into the gut, where microbes in the intestine convert them into secondary bile acids. These secondary bile acids then appear to return to the liver through the portal circulation.\n\nWithin the liver, the figure highlights CXCL16 and CXCR6-positive NKT cells as a protective immune axis. Plus and minus symbols indicate opposing regulation: primary bile acids seem to support CXCL16 expression and recruitment or activity of CXCR6+ NKT cells, whereas secondary bile acids appear to suppress this pathway. Reduced NKT-cell activity is linked to increased tumorigenesis, shown by the tumor cluster and the inhibitory red symbol near that label.\n\nAntibiotic treatment is depicted as acting on the gut side of the system, presumably reducing intestinal bacteria and therefore lowering formation of secondary bile acids. Overall, the image conveys that altering the microbiome can shift bile-acid composition, strengthen liver immune surveillance, and restrain tumor development.",
